NNOOTTEESS
       aarrcchh__pprrccttll() is supported only on Linux/x86‐64 for 64‐bit programs cur‐
       rently.

       The 64‐bit base changes when a new 32‐bit segment selector is loaded.

       AARRCCHH__SSEETT__GGSS is disabled in some kernels.

       Context switches for 64‐bit segment bases are rather expensive.  As  an
       optimization,  if  a  32‐bit TLS base address is used, aarrcchh__pprrccttll() may
       use a real TLS entry as if sseett__tthhrreeaadd__aarreeaa(2) had been called,  instead
       of  manipulating  the  segment  base  register directly.  Memory in the
       first 2 GB of address space can be allocated by using mmmmaapp(2) with  the
       MMAAPP__3322BBIITT flag.

       Because  of  the  aforementioned  optimization,  using aarrcchh__pprrccttll() and
       sseett__tthhrreeaadd__aarreeaa(2) in the same thread is dangerous, as they  may  over‐
       write each other’s TLS entries.

